// Chapter 5, "The Build." Sources read for this chapter (verified 2026-10-16):
// ISACA Certification Exam Candidate Guide, version 1.26 (2026), Appendix A, CISA Examination Content Outline (effective August
//   2024): topics 3A1 project governance and management; 3A2 business case and feasibility analysis; 3A3 system development
//   methodologies; 3A4 control identification and design; 3B1 system readiness and implementation testing. Quiz items 14 (2A5,
//   a residual rating resting on an old control test) and 15 (1B5, reporting a scope limitation) are spaced review of chapter 3:
//   https://www.isaca.org/-/media/files/isacadp/project/isaca/certification/exam-candidate-guides/2026/exam-candidate-guide.pdf
//   https://www.isaca.org/credentialing/cisa/cisa-exam-content-outline
// Earned value (planned value, earned value, actual cost; CPI = EV / AC, SPI = EV / PV, above 1 favorable; EAC = BAC / CPI when
//   the CPI holds): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earned_value_management
// Critical path (longest chain of dependent activities; critical activities have zero total float):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Critical_path_method
// PERT three point estimate, expected time = (optimistic + 4 x most likely + pessimistic) / 6:
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Program_evaluation_and_review_technique
// Function points (a measure of the business functionality software provides to its users; ISO/IEC 14143 family):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_point
// Payback period (time for cash inflows to repay the investment): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Payback_period
// Scrum (product owner manages the product backlog and speaks for stakeholders; sprints):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scrum_(project_management)
// Source code escrow (code deposited with a third party, released to the licensee if the vendor fails or stops maintaining it):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Source_code_escrow
// Acceptance testing (user acceptance testing is run by the business before release, against acceptance criteria):
//   https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acceptance_testing
// Flatiron Flyer (RTD express bus, Boulder to Denver Union Station along US 36): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flatiron_Flyer
// Dates: Wednesday, March 17, 2027. US daylight saving started March 14 and the UK changes March 28, so Denver is UTC minus 6 and six
//   hours behind London: 6:40 AM in Denver is 12:40 PM in London. Kade's board meets Thursday, March 18. 2019 weekdays computed:
//   Tuesday, March 19 (board approves the business case); Monday, April 1 (charter); Friday, August 30 (last status report); Friday,
//   September 13 (go live approval); Saturday, September 14 and Sunday, September 15 (cutover); Monday, September 16 (first day
//   live); Friday, October 4 (UAT sign off, 18 days after go live); Tuesday, January 22 (Pellridge formed, 56 days before
//   March 19). March 17 to March 29, 2027 is twelve days. Saturday, March 20, 2027 is the diner breakfast Henry commits to.
// Figures (drill and quiz, re-derived): business case one off cost $5,000,000, forecast savings $2,000,000 a year, payback
//   5,000,000 / 2,000,000 = 2.5 years. August 30, 2019 status report: PV $4,000,000, EV $3,200,000, AC $5,600,000; CPI = 3.2 / 5.6 =
//   0.5714, shown 0.57; SPI = 3.2 / 4.0 = 0.80; EAC = 5.0 / 0.5714 = $8,750,000. Final cost $7,400,000, payback at that cost
//   7.4 / 2.0 = 3.7 years; overrun (7.4 minus 5.0) / 5.0 = 48 percent. Quiz payback $900,000 / $300,000 = 3 years.
// Facts set here for later chapters: Project Longitude, Meridian's 2019 settlement platform, built and run by Pellridge. Pellridge
//   formed Tuesday, January 22, 2019, eight weeks to the day before the board; the business case (board, March 19, 2019, the meeting that approved the authorization policy) offered one
//   option, Pellridge, with no other vendor asked; benefits owner not named, no benefits review on file. Charter April 1, 2019:
//   sponsor C. Wynn, steering committee chaired by S. Kade, project manager from Pellridge, Pellridge working before its July 2019
//   contract. Steering committee met April, May, and June 2019 only. Gates 3 and 4 waived by the sponsor by email. Pellridge owns
//   the code, Meridian holds a license, no escrow. Requirement R-14 (preparer can't approve) deferred to a release 2 that never
//   came, deferral approved by Pellridge's project manager, compensating control "desk procedure". Threshold and approver list
//   are configuration parameters maintained by Pellridge, change history "release 2". UAT run by Pellridge testers on an unmasked
//   copy of production merchant data; summary signed October 4, 2019 by Pellridge's project manager "for Settlement Operations".
//   Go live approved by C. Wynn by email Friday, September 13, 2019, 6:10 PM; direct cutover, no parallel run, rollback "not
//   required". R-07's 2019 control assessment covered April 8 to 19, 2019, on the legacy system retired September 15, 2019.
//   Kade's foreword: "a team I know well" (no time depth; the bio gap stays book six's). Project file: 186 documents.
//   Maisie came down on the bus; at the diner the waitress turns ch2's upside down mug the right way up for her. Henry
//   commits to Saturday, March 20 at nine, fetching her at eight, and tells Laura himself (back by noon).
(((window.HALCYON_DATA=window.HALCYON_DATA||{}).cisa=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa||{}).full=window.HALCYON_DATA.cisa.full||[]).push({
  num: 5, part: `Part Two: The Dome`, title: `The Build`, weeks: `Wednesday, March 17`,
  tasks: [`3A1`,`3A2`,`3A3`,`3A4`,`3B1`],
  cast: [],
  opening: [
    `By ten to six on Wednesday the overnight batch from counsel was sitting in Henry's inbox: Meridian's 2019 project file, a hundred and eighty six documents, in whatever order somebody's software had found them. The project had been called Longitude. A company named Meridian had called its big project Longitude, and somebody in 2019 had been very pleased with that.`,
    `He sorted the file by date before he opened anything. Projects tell you two stories, the order things were signed and the order they should have been signed, and the second one is usually shorter.`,
    `At 6:40, with London at lunch, he sent Giles the midweek note. Most of it was a list. One paragraph wasn't.`,
    `<prop type="email" from="Henry Ashby" to="Giles Fenwick" subject="Midweek" time="6:40 AM">Federal Reserve totals: not received. Meridian hasn't signed its own bank's request. Until they come, the March 3 and 4 breaks can't be tested against a record Meridian didn't keep. If they don't come, the report says so, as a scope limitation imposed by the insured, with the dates.</prop>`,
    `Kade's board would meet tomorrow at nine. Henry had no idea what would be in its pack, and it wasn't his business to find out.`,
    `At 7:02 his phone buzzed.`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="7:02 AM">can i come down today. on the bus. mum says ask you not her so im asking</prop>`,
    `Most of what she'd ever wanted from him had come through Laura. He wrote <b>Yes. I'll meet the bus.</b> Then, because a woman on Pearl Street had told him on Monday what not being told things was like, he sent Laura a line as well. <b>Maisie's coming down on the bus today. I'll put her on the four o'clock back.</b>`,
    `<prop type="text" from="Laura" time="7:09 AM">I know. She asked me first. I told her to ask you.</prop>`,
    `He read the word "you" and put the phone face down and opened the file.`
  ],
  lesson: {
    title: `Dot's Workpapers: the build`,
    sections: [
      { h: `Somebody has to own it`,
        body: [`Every project I ever examined had a plan, Lime Street. Very few had an owner. Start there. The sponsor is the senior person in the business who wants the thing built, pays for it, and answers for whether it was worth it. The steering committee is a few senior people from the business and from IT who make the big calls: scope, money, and whether to stop. The project manager runs the work day to day and reports to them. The project charter says all of that in writing before anyone spends a dollar: the objectives, the scope, the budget, who decides what, and what counts as done.`,
          `Then the gates. A well run project stops at fixed points, at the end of requirements, design, and testing, and someone with authority looks at the evidence and decides whether it goes on. A waived gate is a decision as well. Find out who waived it, and get it in writing.`,
          `Project managers have tools, and you should know what each one tells you. A Gantt chart shows tasks against dates. The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks through the plan. Those tasks have no slack, so a day lost on any of them is a day lost on the whole project. PERT estimates a task from three guesses, optimistic, most likely, and pessimistic, and weights the middle one: the optimistic, plus four times the most likely, plus the pessimistic, all divided by six. Function point analysis sizes software by what it does for its users, its inputs, outputs, inquiries, files, and interfaces, so the estimate doesn't depend on the programming language. Timebox management fixes the date and lets the scope give way, which is fine, provided somebody decides what gives.`,
          `Earned value is the one I'd make you learn by heart. Planned value is the budgeted cost of the work that should be done by now. Earned value is the budgeted cost of the work that actually is done. Actual cost is what's been spent. Divide earned value by actual cost and you have the cost performance index. Divide earned value by planned value and you have the schedule performance index. Below one is bad on both. A project at 0.6 on cost is buying sixty cents of work with every dollar, and no color on a status report changes that.`,
          `Your job on a project is to look at whether it's governed and whether the controls are being built, at the gates and at the end. You can advise. You don't run it, and you don't sign its gates, or you'll be auditing your own signature later.`],
        terms: [[`Project sponsor`,`The senior business owner who funds the project, owns the business case, and answers for the benefits.`],[`Project steering committee`,`Senior business and IT people who make the project's major decisions, including whether it continues.`],[`Critical path`,`The longest chain of dependent tasks in a schedule. Its tasks have zero slack.`],[`CPI and SPI`,`Cost performance index, earned value divided by actual cost. Schedule performance index, earned value divided by planned value. Below 1 is unfavorable.`],[`Function point analysis`,`Sizing software by the functions it delivers to users, independent of the programming language.`]],
        exam: `The sponsor owns the business case and the benefits, and the steering committee makes the major calls, including stopping. CPI is EV divided by AC and SPI is EV divided by PV. Below 1 means over budget or behind schedule. Critical path tasks have zero slack. Function points measure size independent of language.` },
      { h: `Why are we doing this`,
        body: [`A business case says why the company should spend the money. It names the problem or the opportunity, the options including doing nothing, what each option costs to build and to run, what it brings back, the risks, and who will answer for the benefits. A feasibility study comes first or alongside, and asks whether it can be done at all: technically, financially, operationally, legally, and in the time there is.`,
          `The money has a few standard measures. Payback is how long the savings take to repay the cost: five million spent, two million a year saved, two and a half years. Return on investment compares the gain with the cost. Net present value discounts future savings back into today's money, because a dollar in five years is worth less than a dollar now. The arithmetic is easy, and it isn't where business cases go wrong. They go wrong in the inputs. Whose estimate is the saving, and who's going to check it?`,
          `Read the options with particular care. A case with one real option and a straw man beside it isn't a choice. It's a decision looking for a signature. Ask what the alternatives were, whether anyone outside the company was asked to bid, and who checked the winner's claims about itself.`,
          `And a business case isn't finished when the board approves it. It should be looked at again at every gate, because costs rise and benefits shrink, and a project that no longer pays should be stopped by somebody. After go live, the benefits get measured against the forecast by the business owner who promised them. If the case names no owner, nobody will ever measure them, and the case turns into a story the company once told itself.`],
        terms: [[`Business case`,`The justification for a project: need, options, costs, benefits, risks, and who owns the benefits.`],[`Feasibility study`,`An assessment of whether a proposed solution is technically, financially, operationally, and legally workable, and worth doing.`],[`Payback period`,`The time it takes for a project's savings or returns to repay its cost.`],[`Net present value (NPV)`,`Future cash flows discounted to today's value, minus the cost. Positive means the project adds value.`],[`Benefits realization`,`Measuring, after implementation, whether the promised benefits arrived. The business owner's job.`]],
        exam: `The business case belongs to the sponsor and is revisited at each major decision point. Benefits are measured after implementation, against the case, by the business. A case that considers only one option hasn't shown that the choice was the best one.` },
      { h: `How it gets built`,
        body: [`There are several ways to build a system, and the exam wants you to know them by their habits. The traditional life cycle, usually called waterfall, runs in phases: feasibility, requirements, design, build, test, implementation, and a review afterward. Each phase finishes and is approved before the next begins. It suits work whose requirements are stable and well understood. Its weakness is that users see the system late, when changes are expensive.`,
          `Agile methods build in short iterations, scrum's sprints of a few weeks, each ending in working software. A product owner speaks for the business and ranks the backlog, the list of what's still to be built. Documentation is lighter, and it ought to be. That's no excuse for having none. The controls a phase gate gave you still have to live somewhere: acceptance criteria on each item, a definition of done, and a record of who decided what fell off the backlog.`,
          `Prototyping and rapid application development build a rough version quickly so that users can react to it. The danger is that the rough version becomes the real one, without the controls it was never meant to have. DevOps puts development and operations together and releases often, with automated testing and deployment. It can be very well controlled, and it can be very badly controlled very quickly. Reverse engineering works backward from a finished program to understand it, and licenses often forbid it.`,
          `Then building against buying. A package should be chosen against written requirements, usually through a request for proposal, with the references checked. When a vendor writes the software and keeps the code, the company should have a source code escrow agreement, so that if the vendor fails, the code comes to the company and it can carry on. Whatever the method, I check the same things. Were the requirements written down and approved? Can each one be traced through design, build, and test? Who approved the changes? Did the business accept the result?`],
        terms: [[`System development life cycle (SDLC)`,`The phased approach to building systems, from feasibility through post implementation review. Waterfall is its traditional, sequential form.`],[`Agile and scrum`,`Iterative development in short sprints. The product owner represents the business and prioritizes the backlog.`],[`Prototyping and RAD`,`Building quick working versions for users to react to. Risk: a prototype moves into production without controls.`],[`Source code escrow`,`The vendor's source code held by a third party and released to the customer if the vendor fails or stops supporting it.`]],
        exam: `In agile, the product owner represents the business and prioritizes the backlog. Waterfall fits stable, well understood requirements. Whatever the method, the auditor looks for approved requirements, traceability, controlled changes, and business acceptance. Escrow protects the customer when a vendor fails.` },
      { h: `Controls go in at the drawing board`,
        body: [`A control designed into a system costs a little. The same control bolted on after go live costs a great deal, and usually works worse. So control requirements belong in the requirements, written by the business owner with security and audit's advice, and traced through design, build, and test like any other requirement. A requirements traceability matrix does the tracing. Read it across, one requirement at a time, and look for the rows that stop partway.`,
          `Know the application controls by type. Input controls make sure only valid, authorized data gets in: a range check, a limit check, a reasonableness check, a check digit on an account number, a completeness check on the required fields. Processing controls make sure it's handled correctly once it's in: run to run totals carried from one step to the next, reconciliations, edit checks. Output controls make sure the results are complete and reach only the people who should have them. Under all of them, an audit trail records who did what, and when.`,
          `The best controls are enforced by the system, not promised by a procedure. If the standard says the person who prepared a payment can't approve it, the system should refuse. A desk procedure that says please don't is a compensating control, and a weak one. And watch for controls that are really settings: a threshold in a table, a list of approvers. A control like that is only as good as the protection on the table. Who can change it, who approves the change, and is the change logged somewhere the person changing it can't reach?`,
          `When a control is dropped from a release, the decision belongs to the business owner who carries the risk. It's written down, with what compensates and when the control comes back. A project manager can't accept a risk on the business's behalf, and a vendor certainly can't accept one for its customer.`,
          `Your part in design is to review and advise. Say what a control should achieve, and look at whether the design achieves it. Don't design it yourself, and don't sign it off. If you design a control, you've given up the right to audit it.`],
        terms: [[`Requirements traceability matrix`,`A table linking each requirement to its design, build, and test, so you can see where any requirement stopped.`],[`Input controls`,`Checks that only valid, complete, authorized data enters a system: range, limit, reasonableness, check digit, completeness.`],[`Processing controls`,`Checks that data is processed completely and accurately, such as run to run totals and reconciliations.`],[`Audit trail`,`A record of who did what in a system, and when, protected from the people it records.`],[`Compensating control`,`A control that reduces a risk when the intended control isn't in place. Usually weaker than the one it replaces.`]],
        exam: `Controls are cheapest and most effective when designed in at requirements and design. A system enforced control is stronger than a procedural one. Changes to control settings should be authorized and logged. The auditor advises on control requirements but doesn't design or approve them.` },
      { h: `Before it goes live`,
        body: [`Testing comes in layers. Unit testing checks each piece of code on its own, usually by the developer who wrote it. Integration testing checks that the pieces pass data to each other correctly. System testing checks the whole thing against its specification, including how it behaves under load, how it recovers, and how secure it is. Regression testing reruns earlier tests after a change, to show that nothing which used to work has broken.`,
          `Then user acceptance testing, the layer people cut corners on. UAT is the business testing the system against its own requirements, with its own people, before go live. It's planned in advance, with test cases tied to the requirements and expected results written down before anyone runs them. The business owner signs the results. A UAT signed after go live is a receipt. A UAT signed by the vendor who built the system is the vendor's opinion of its own work.`,
          `Test data is a question of its own. Testing on a copy of production data is realistic and dangerous, because test environments are rarely protected like production and the data belongs to real people. Use made up data where you can. Where you can't, mask it, protect it, and destroy it when the testing's done.`,
          `Then the changeover. In a parallel run, the old and new systems both process the same work for a while and the results are compared. It's the safest way and the dearest. A pilot puts the new system in one place first. A phased changeover moves one piece at a time. A direct changeover switches the old one off and the new one on in a single weekend. If you do that, you'd better have a way back, and the go live decision should be made against written readiness criteria by someone with the authority to make it. Some organizations call the technical evaluation certification, and management's formal acceptance of the system and its remaining risks accreditation. The names vary. The point is that someone senior accepts it, in writing, before it goes live.`,
          `One more thing. A control tested on the old system tells you about the old system. When the system changes, the testing starts again.`],
        terms: [[`Integration testing`,`Testing the interfaces between components, to confirm data passes between them correctly.`],[`User acceptance testing (UAT)`,`Testing by the business against its requirements before go live, signed off by the business owner.`],[`Regression testing`,`Rerunning earlier tests after a change to confirm existing functions still work.`],[`Changeover approaches`,`Parallel (old and new run side by side), pilot (one site first), phased (one piece at a time), direct or abrupt (switch over at once).`],[`Accreditation`,`Management's formal acceptance of a system and its residual risks before it goes into production.`]],
        exam: `UAT is run by users against business requirements and signed off by the business owner before go live. Regression testing checks that a change didn't break what already worked. Production data used in testing must be masked or protected. A parallel run gives the most assurance at changeover.` }
    ]
  },
  scenes: [
    { id:`f5s1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A1`, title:`Whose project`, q:`The project charter for Longitude names Pellridge as project manager and no business owner. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[...(G.hidconflict
        ?[`Priyanka's review of Monday and Tuesday had come in at ten to five, which was the middle of her morning. Nine notes, numbered, in the dry style she used for people she'd decided to take seriously. Seven were fair. He cleared those first and wrote his answers under the other two, so she could see he'd read them.`]
        :[]),...((s.flags.toldkade||G.toldkade)
        ?[`At 7:15 counsel forwarded one more thing from the overnight batch: Meridian's vendor register, sent again "with corrections." In the Pellridge row, Right to audit now said Yes. There was no date beside it.`,
          `He laid it next to the copy of the Pellridge contract counsel had sent him on Monday morning, nine pages with no audit right anywhere in them, and next to the addendum dated Monday at 4:15 PM. Then he wrote the test into the file. Contract as received Monday morning: no right to audit. Addendum signed Monday afternoon, less than three hours after he'd talked over coffee. Register re-sent Wednesday: Yes, undated. In force from March 2 to March 5: no right to audit. The register had been corrected. The only record of what it had said on the day that mattered was now in his file, and he knew exactly whose fault that was.`]
        :[`At 7:15 he went through the rest of the overnight batch. There was nothing from Meridian about Pellridge. The vendor register in his file was still Monday's, and its Pellridge row still said none.`]),
        `Then the project charter, the oldest document in the file that anyone had signed.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, project charter, April 1, 2019 (extract)">Objective: replace the settlement platform by Q3 2019.<br>Sponsor: C. Wynn, CFO. Steering committee: S. Kade (chair), C. Wynn, CIO, Head of Settlement Operations.<br>Project manager: Pellridge Data Services (contract in preparation).<br>Method: Meridian SDLC, five phase gates, each approved by the sponsor.<br>Business owner for acceptance: not stated.</prop>`,`The Pellridge contract was signed in July 2019. By the look of this, Pellridge had been running the project since April.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rate the project's governance by its result, seven years live.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes "delivered and operating." It's true. It says nothing about who decided what along the way, which is the only reason anyone opens a project file seven years later.`,
          why:`A system that runs isn't evidence that the project was governed. Governance is about who held authority and made the decisions, and the outcome doesn't tell you either.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for the steering minutes before he forms any view.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`They're already in the batch, two folders down. It's a sensible instinct. But the charter itself has already told him something, and he hasn't written it down.`,
          why:`The minutes will matter. The charter already shows who held each role, and that the vendor managed its own delivery. Record that now.`},
        {t:`Map each project role to the person or firm that held it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He draws it on a fresh page. Sponsor: the CFO. Steering committee: chaired by the chief executive. Project manager: the vendor that was building the system, working three months before it had a contract. Business owner for acceptance: nobody. So the people who would build Longitude also reported on how the building was going, and nobody on Meridian's side was named to say whether it was any good.`,
          why:`Project governance starts with the roles: a sponsor who owns the case, a steering committee that decides, a project manager accountable to them, and a business owner who accepts the result. A vendor managing its own delivery, with no business owner named, leaves nobody independent watching it.`},
        {t:`Ring Pellridge's old project manager and ask how it was run.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Counsel stops him at the word "ring." Pellridge is Meridian's vendor, and its project manager's memory of 2019 is the vendor's account of its own work, by phone, years later.`,
          why:`Contact with the insured's vendors goes through the agreed channel. And the vendor's recollection is the weakest evidence about a project the vendor ran. Start with the documents.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s2`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A1`, title:`Green`, q:`Every monthly status report Pellridge sent the sponsor has a green circle on top. How should Henry read it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At half past eight he opened the steering committee folder. It was thin. There were minutes for April, May, and June 2019, each a page long, each ending "next meeting in one month." After June there was nothing, and the project still had three months to run.`,`What there was, after June, was a status report a month from Pellridge's project manager to the sponsor. Every one of them had a large green circle at the top. He went to the last one.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, monthly status report, Friday, August 30, 2019 (extract)">Overall status: GREEN.<br>Budget at completion: $5,000,000.<br>Planned value to date: $4,000,000. Earned value to date: $3,200,000. Actual cost to date: $5,600,000.<br>Go live: on track for September 16.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Record the status as the report gives it: green, every month.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He writes "project reported on track." The figures underneath the green circle say otherwise, and he hasn't looked at them.`,
          why:`A status color is the reporter's summary. The figures in the same report are evidence, and they should be checked against the summary.`},
        {t:`Work out cost and schedule performance from the report's own figures.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`Earned value over actual cost: 3.2 over 5.6, about 0.57 on cost. Earned value over planned value: 0.80 on schedule. Meridian had paid for more than the whole budget and got about two thirds of the work. The report called it green, and the only person reading it was the sponsor, because the committee that was supposed to decide what to do about a project like this had stopped meeting in June.`,
          why:`Earned value lets you test a status report against its own numbers. CPI is EV divided by AC and SPI is EV divided by PV, and both were well below 1. Bad numbers under a green summary, with the steering committee gone, mean nobody with authority was deciding.`},
        {t:`Ask why the steering committee stopped meeting after June.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He puts it on counsel's list. It's a good question, and Meridian will take a week over the answer. Meanwhile the report on his desk already says what the committee missed, and he hasn't worked it out.`,
          why:`The reason the committee stopped matters, and asking is fine. The report's own earned value figures are evidence you can use today.`},
        {t:`Request Pellridge's 2019 invoices to trace where the money went.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Counsel adds it to the list, and it'll be a week. The invoices will show what Pellridge billed. The report on his desk already shows what Meridian got for it, and he hasn't worked that out.`,
          why:`Invoices can explain an overrun later. The earned value figures in the report test its green status today, and that comes first.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s3`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A2`, title:`The case for Longitude`, q:`The business case for Longitude promised savings, fewer errors and triple capacity. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The policy in Leonard's folder said only "Approved by the Board, March 2019." Last night's batch had the minutes. Tuesday, March 19, 2019: the settlement authorization policy at item four, the one nobody had reviewed since, and Longitude's business case at item five.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, business case, March 2019 (extract)">One off cost: $5,000,000.<br>Benefits: IT running costs down $2,000,000 a year from 2020 (in house IT team reduced from 16 to 5). Settlement errors down 80 percent. Capacity tripled.<br>Payback: 2.5 years.<br>Benefits owner: not stated. Benefits review: to be scheduled.</prop>`,`He asked counsel for anything that measured those benefits after 2019. The answer came back in twenty minutes. There wasn't anything.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rely on the board's approval of the business case in March 2019.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He notes "approved by the board, March 2019." It was approved. Nobody ever found out whether it was right.`,
          why:`Approval shows a decision was made. It doesn't show the case was sound, or that its benefits arrived. Both are things to test.`},
        {t:`Rework the payback at the final cost of the project.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`At the final cost the payback stretches to 3.7 years. It's useful arithmetic. It also assumes the savings were real, and nobody ever checked whether they were.`,
          why:`Recalculating with actual costs is worth doing. The bigger gap is that no one owned the benefits or measured them, so the whole case rests on a forecast nobody ever tested.`},
        {t:`Record that the benefits had no owner and were never measured.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it plainly. A five million dollar case, with savings of two million a year promised from 2020. No benefits owner named. No benefits review, ever. The only benefit anyone at Meridian can show him is the one that cost them the most: the IT team went from sixteen to five, and the work went to Pellridge.`,
          why:`A business case should name an owner for its benefits, and those benefits should be measured after implementation against the forecast. If nobody owns them, nobody measures them, and the organization never learns whether the investment paid.`},
        {t:`Ask the CFO, as sponsor, for the benefits Longitude delivered.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`Counsel words the request. The answer will come from the sponsor, in the middle of a claim, with figures nobody collected at the time. The file already says no one owned the benefits or measured them.`,
          why:`Management's account after the event isn't a benefits review. The case named no benefits owner and no review was ever held, and that's the finding.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s4`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A2`, title:`One option`, q:`Kade's foreword says he trusts the team building Longitude completely. What should Henry make of the options section?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The options section was a page and a half. The foreword above it was signed.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, business case, foreword and options (extract)">"This is the platform that will carry Meridian for the next twenty years. I've asked a team I know well to build it, and I trust them completely. S. Kade."<br><br>Option A: do nothing. Not viable.<br>Option B: Pellridge Data Services to build and operate. Recommended.<br>Other vendors approached: none. Technical feasibility: confirmed by Pellridge.</prop>`,`He went back to the filing he'd pulled on Tuesday, for the line he'd only noted the year of. Formed Tuesday, January 22, 2019. Eight weeks to the day before it was the only option in front of Meridian's board.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report the choice as steered toward a vendor Kade knew well.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It might have been. What the document shows is a weak decision, made on one vendor's word. Steered is a claim about people's intentions, and he has none of their intentions on paper.`,
          why:`A feasibility study that considers one option is a weakness in the decision. It isn't proof of motive. Report the gap, and keep conclusions about intent out of it.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel whether any other vendor was ever approached.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel asks. The document already answers it, in the line that says "none," and the answer that comes back will only repeat it.`,
          why:`Confirming is reasonable. But the case states that no other vendor was approached and that the vendor confirmed its own feasibility. That's already a finding.`},
        {t:`Record that the study weighed one vendor against doing nothing.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it as a finding about the decision. A five million dollar choice between one vendor and nothing. No request for proposals, no other bids, no references, and the vendor's feasibility confirmed by the vendor. The vendor was eight weeks old. Then, on a separate line, because it belongs with the related party column he'd looked at on Tuesday and not with this finding: the chief executive's foreword says he knows the team well.`,
          why:`A feasibility study should compare real alternatives, test the chosen option's claims independently, and check the vendor's capability. One option against doing nothing, on the vendor's own word, isn't a basis for a sound decision.`},
        {t:`Get his own quotes from other vendors for the same platform.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`He'd be building a business case of his own, eight years late, for a decision he doesn't own. It would prove nothing about what Meridian considered in 2019.`,
          why:`Evaluate the organization's decision process with its own records. Producing your own alternatives is consulting work, and it isn't evidence of what management did.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s5`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A3`, title:`Two week sprints`, q:`Pellridge's notes describe a different method from the one the charter promised. What should Henry make of it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At ten past ten he found the method. The charter promised Meridian's own life cycle, five phase gates, each approved by the sponsor. Pellridge's progress notes from May onward described something else.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, method (extracts)">Pellridge progress note, May 2019: "Delivery moved to two week sprints. Backlog maintained by Pellridge."<br>Email, C. Wynn to Pellridge, July 22, 2019: "Gates 3 and 4 waived to protect the date. Carry on."<br>Requirements baseline: last approved version, April 2019. Backlog items deferred to release 2 at go live: 41.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Report the switch to sprints as unsuitable for a payment system.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He writes it, and it reads like an opinion about fashion. Plenty of payment systems are built in sprints, and some of them are well controlled.`,
          why:`No method is a control failure in itself. The question is whether the controls the organization needed were kept, in whatever form the method allows.`},
        {t:`Leave the delivery method to Pellridge as the firm doing the work.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He moves on. Forty one pieces of the system were deferred, and the person deciding which ones was the vendor.`,
          why:`The method is how the organization's requirements get built and approved. When it changes, the organization should decide how its controls carry over.`},
        {t:`Ask the sponsor why the method was changed partway through.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`Counsel adds it to the list. It'll be useful to have. But the reason won't change what the email and the backlog already show.`,
          why:`The reason is worth recording, but the evidence of what was lost is already in the file. Assess that first.`},
        {t:`Look for the agile controls that should have replaced the gates.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He goes looking for what took the gates' place. Acceptance criteria on the backlog items: none. A product owner from the business: none, since the backlog was Pellridge's. A record of who approved the forty one deferrals: none. The sprints weren't the problem. The problem was that when the gates went, nothing went in where they'd been.`,
          why:`Agile can be well controlled if its own controls are in place: a product owner for the business, acceptance criteria, a definition of done, and recorded decisions about scope. Waiving the gates without putting those in removes the approvals and puts nothing in their place.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s6`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A3`, title:`Licensed, not owned`, q:`The contract leaves Longitude's software Pellridge's property, licensed to Meridian. How should Henry treat that?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At twenty to eleven he went back to the Pellridge contract, the nine pages he'd read on Tuesday, and went back to the clause he'd passed over then as boilerplate.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Pellridge master services agreement, July 2019, intellectual property (extract)">All software developed under this agreement remains the property of Pellridge. Meridian receives a license to use it for the term of the agreement.<br>Source code escrow: not addressed.<br>System documentation: maintained by Pellridge, available to Meridian on request.</prop>`,`Counsel confirmed by email that Meridian had never asked for the documentation.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave the clause to Meridian's lawyers as a contract question.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He leaves it for the lawyers. If Pellridge stops answering the phone tomorrow, Meridian can't change a line of its own settlement system, and the claim's restoration costs rest on exactly that system.`,
          why:`Who owns and can maintain a critical system bears on continuity and on any restoration claim. It's part of how the system was acquired, and it belongs in the assessment.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel whether Meridian holds its system documentation.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It doesn't, counsel has already said. Asking again won't help. The finding is in the clause itself.`,
          why:`Documentation matters, and checking for it is fine. But the contract already shows the bigger exposure: no ownership of the code, and no escrow.`},
        {t:`Note Meridian can't maintain its core system without Pellridge.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it as a dependency nobody chose. Pellridge owns the code. Meridian holds a license, no escrow, and no documentation it has ever asked for. Meridian's own IT team was five people, none of whom had built it. If the vendor failed or walked away, the company would be left with a system it couldn't change, and nobody at Meridian appears to have noticed.`,
          why:`When a vendor builds and keeps the code for a critical system, the customer needs escrow, and enough documentation and in house skill to carry on. Without them the organization depends entirely on one vendor, which is a risk to continuity and to control.`},
        {t:`Recommend Meridian buy the source code from Pellridge now.`, s:0, d:{trust:-1,conf:0,health:-2},
          after:`It's a recommendation, and possibly a good one, for a finding he hasn't written yet.`,
          why:`Record the condition and its risk first. Recommendations follow findings, and how to fix it is management's decision.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s7`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A4`, title:`R-14`, q:`In the traceability matrix, the preparer rule was deferred by Pellridge. What should Henry do with it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At five to eleven he opened the requirements traceability matrix, a spreadsheet four hundred rows long, and searched it for the words from the standard he'd read on Friday: did not prepare the file.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, requirements traceability matrix, row R-14">Requirement: "The system shall prevent the user who prepared a settlement file from approving it." Source: Settlement Authorization Standard, section 2.<br>Design: deferred to release 2. Test: UAT-031, not run (deferred).<br>Deferral approved by: project manager (Pellridge), August 2019.<br>Compensating control: desk procedure.<br>Release 2: not started.</prop>`,`He had twenty five minutes before the bus. He used ten of them on this row.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rely on the desk procedure named as the compensating control.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He writes "deferred, with compensating control." The compensating control is a page of instructions, and he's already seen what happened to it in January 2026.`,
          why:`A deferral is a risk acceptance, and it has to be made by the business owner who carries the risk. A procedure standing in for a system control is weak, and needs testing on its own terms.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for Meridian's current plans to build release 2.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Counsel sends the request. The plans, if there are any, will describe the future. The finding is about the years the system has run without the control since 2019.`,
          why:`Plans to fix the gap are management's business and may be worth knowing. They don't change the condition: the control was required and never built.`},
        {t:`Follow R-14 from requirement to test to whoever approved it.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`He follows the row across. The standard required it. The requirement was written down. The design deferred it, the test was never run, and release 2 never came. So since 2019 the system has never once refused a preparer who tried to approve their own file. The deferral was approved by the vendor's project manager, which means the vendor accepted a risk on Meridian's behalf. The compensating control was the desk procedure, and the January 2026 procedure on his desk since Friday had dropped those very words.`,
          why:`Read the traceability matrix across each row to see where a requirement stopped. A key control deferred from the build, by someone with no authority to accept the risk, and covered only by a procedure, is a design failure, and more serious when the procedure itself later changed.`},
        {t:`Link the gap to the 2,906 settlements just under the line.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It's a lovely theory, and he has no evidence at all that any of the 2,906 were prepared and approved by the same person.`,
          why:`A design gap shows what the system allowed. It isn't evidence of what anyone did. Keep the weakness and the theory apart until the evidence joins them.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s8`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A4`, title:`Terrible tea`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p4=((all&&all[4]&&all[4].picks)||{})[6];const missed=!!G.missedfair;
        return [`At twenty past eleven he was at the bottom of the escalators in the bus concourse under Union Station with two hot chocolates nobody had asked for. Maisie came down with her hood up and a backpack on one shoulder, looked at the cups, and took one.`,`He'd thought about lunch since seven. There was a soup place on Wazee that was perfectly good. He took her up Colfax instead, in a cab, to the diner.`,`The last time he'd sat in the booth by the window was a Wednesday too, a week ago, and the waitress had turned the second mug upside down. She came over with the pot, looked at Maisie, and turned it the right way up without a word.`,`He sat where he always sat. Then he got up and moved to the other side, her side, and gave Maisie his.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Is this her place?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Saturdays. Nine months of them."</span>`,
        (missed
          ?`Maisie put her phone on the table and turned it round to face him. It was a photo of the poster, the whole thing, taken square on. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Mum said you'd ask to see it. You didn't. So."</span> He looked at it properly, every chart and the error in the corner, for long enough that she started to look embarrassed, and then for a bit longer. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"It's good,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"I know,"</span> she said, and put the phone away, but slowly.`
          :`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"The poster's still on my door,"</span> she said. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Mum says it's a fire hazard. It's not, it's cardboard. Well. It is a bit."</span>`),
        ...((p4===3||p4===0)
          ?[`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Did you get a toaster?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Not yet."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You've got twelve days."</span>`]
          :[]),
        `Then she said, <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You said another time. About her."</span>`,`So he told her. The fork, and what Dot had said about how he held it. The green pen she wouldn't lend anyone. The sugar packets, torn into strips and lined up while she listened, so that by the end of breakfast there was a little fence of paper on her side of the table. Maisie laughed at the fork. She didn't laugh at the sugar.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"She asked about you,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Every Saturday. Before she'd looked at the menu."</span>`,`Maisie looked at the menu. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"What did you tell her?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Whatever I knew. Most weeks it wasn't much."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You could've asked me."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I know."</span>`,`She thought about that, and then about something else. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"What did she have?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Tea. She said it was terrible every week, and ordered it every week."</span>`,`Maisie ordered the tea. It came, and it was terrible, and she made a face at it that Dot had made every Saturday, a face Maisie had never seen. His phone buzzed on the table. Meridian. She nodded at it. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Go on. I've got tea."</span>`,`It was Leonard Pratt, and he sounded as if he'd been up early. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Sorry, I know it's lunch. Carla wants a remediation plan in tomorrow's board pack for the controls Longitude never got, and she's asked me to write it, since I'm the controls person. I've drafted the design. A system block on the preparer, logging on the settings, the lot. I saw your request for the traceability matrix, so I know you've been looking. Could you read it tonight and tell me if it would pass? Ten minutes of your time."</span>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Talk him through a design that would work for the board.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:-3,health:-3},
          after:`It takes twenty minutes. Leonard writes fast and thanks him often. By the time Henry puts the phone down, Maisie has finished her tea and most of his water, and tomorrow Meridian's board will approve a design with Henry's thinking all through it, which he'll then have to audit.`,
          why:`Designing the auditee's controls is a management function. It creates a self review threat, and in the middle of a claims investigation it compromises the independence the work depends on.`},
        {t:`Read it tonight on an informal basis and sign nothing.`, s:1, d:{trust:-1,conf:-1,health:0},
          after:`Leonard sends it at seven. Informal lasts until the board pack, where a footnote says the plan was "discussed with the insurer's investigator." He'll spend Thursday asking counsel to get the footnote taken out.`,
          why:`An informal review still connects you to the design and can be cited as endorsement. Advising on what controls should achieve is fine. Telling the auditee whether its design would pass, in the middle of your own work on the same control, isn't.`},
        {t:`Decline, and tell him the design has to be Meridian's own.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:3,health:4},
          after:`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I can't, Leonard. It's Meridian's design, and the board should hear it from Meridian. If I mark it, I'm part of it, and in a month I'll be writing about whether it works."</span> A pause. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"And if internal audit designs the control, who audits it?"</span> Leonard is quiet for long enough that Henry thinks the call has dropped. <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Nobody,"</span> he says. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Might be worth saying that to Carla. In writing."</span> He's back in the booth in four minutes. Maisie has drunk half his tea.`,
          why:`The auditor's role in design is to advise on what controls should achieve, not to design or approve them. Doing so would impair independence for the audit that follows. And internal audit designing the controls it will later audit has the same problem.`},
        {t:`Tell him to hold the plan back until the claim is settled.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:-1,health:-2},
          after:`Leonard says, slowly, <span class="said" data-who="Leonard">"Is that a finding?"</span> It isn't, and Henry has just told the auditee what to put in front of its own board, and implied why.`,
          why:`What management takes to its board is management's decision. Directing it interferes with the auditee's governance and signals what you've found.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s9`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3A4`, title:`A number in a table`, q:`Longitude's design spec shows how second approval is set up. How should Henry read it?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>{const p4=((all&&all[4]&&all[4].picks)||{})[6];
        return [`Back on Wazee Street Maisie took the client chair, put her feet on the radiator, and opened a paperback with a dragon on it. Henry turned his screen away from the chair without thinking. She noticed, and approved, in the way she approved of things, by not mentioning it.`,`After a while she got up and stood at the whiteboard. ${(p4===3||p4===0)?`She took the cap off his marker and wrote TOASTER at the top of the shopping list nobody had erased, and underlined it twice.`:`She read the shopping list nobody had erased, all of it, as if it might be evidence.`} Then she looked at the years under it.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"What's 1994?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Something she wrote. I don't know yet."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Okay."</span> She went back to the dragon.`,`He opened Longitude's design specification at the section on authorization.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, design specification, section 6 (extract)">Second approval threshold: configuration setting, default $10,000.<br>Authorized approver list: configuration setting.<br>Settings maintained by: system administrator (Pellridge).<br>Change history for configuration settings: release 2.</prop>`]},
      opts:[
        {t:`Record the $10,000 threshold as a control designed into the system.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He ticks it. The design says a number lives in a table. It doesn't say who can change the number, or whether anyone would know.`,
          why:`A control that depends on a setting is only as strong as the protection over the setting. Check who can change it, how changes are approved, and whether they're logged.`},
        {t:`Ask counsel for today's values of the threshold and the approver list.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`He'll get them. They'll show the settings as they are today. The claim turns on March 2 to 5, and the design says there's no history of what they were then.`,
          why:`Current values are worth having, but a point in time snapshot can't show what the settings were on the dates that matter. The missing change history is the finding.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge to export the settings' history for March 2 to 5.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`Counsel points out two things. Pellridge is reached through Meridian. And the design says there's no history to export.`,
          why:`Requests go through the agreed channel. And a vendor's export about settings it controls is the vendor's own record, not independent evidence.`},
        {t:`Check who can change the threshold and whether changes are logged.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it beside R-14. The threshold that defines the capstone, and the list of who may approve, are settings in a table. The vendor administers the table. No change to it was ever logged, because logging was another thing put off to release 2. So nobody, not even Meridian, can show what the line was on March 5, or who was on the list. He asks counsel for whatever exists, and for a statement of how the settings are protected.`,
          why:`Configurable controls need their settings protected: changes authorized, logged, and reviewed by someone independent of the person making them. A key threshold changeable by a vendor, with no audit trail, is a control designed with a hole in it.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s10`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3B1`, title:`Signed after`, q:`Longitude's acceptance test summary shows 197 of 212 cases passed. What should Henry conclude?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At ten to four he walked her back to Union Station. At the top of the escalator she stopped, with people going round her on both sides.`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Do you still go? Saturdays?"</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I haven't."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You could. I could come."</span> She said it to the escalator, not to him.${(s.picks||{})[7]===0?` A beat. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"If you don't take calls."</span>`:``}`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"Nine o'clock,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I'll come up for you at eight."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"You don't have to."</span>`,`<span class="said" data-who="Henry">"I know,"</span> he said. <span class="said" data-who="Maisie">"Okay,"</span> she said, and went down without looking back, which at fourteen was the same as waving.`,`At twenty past four he was back at the desk with the user acceptance test summary.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, user acceptance testing, summary sign off">Test cases: 212. Passed: 197. Failed: 4 (fixed). Not run: 11.<br>Testers: Pellridge test team.<br>Accepted for Settlement Operations by: project manager (Pellridge).<br>Date: Friday, October 4, 2019.</prop>`,`Go live had been Monday, September 16, 2019.`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Rely on the signed summary: 197 of 212 cases passed.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He writes "UAT completed and signed." It was completed, by the vendor, and signed, by the vendor, nearly three weeks after the system went live.`,
          why:`A UAT sign off is evidence of business acceptance only if the business gave it, before go live. Check who signed, for whom, and when.`},
        {t:`Ask the head of settlement ops if they agreed at the time.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:0},
          after:`Counsel sends it. The answer will be someone's memory of 2019, given in 2027, in the middle of a claim. It won't change the date on the page.`,
          why:`Inquiry may help explain what happened, but it's weak, late evidence. The document already shows that acceptance came from the vendor, after go live.`},
        {t:`Check who signed off and if it came before go live.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes it in two halves. Who: the vendor's project manager signed "for Settlement Operations," and nobody from settlement operations signed at all. When: October 4, eighteen days after the system started moving real money. Eleven cases were never run, and he already knows the number of one of them. So Meridian put its settlement platform into production without the business ever saying it was fit for use.`,
          why:`UAT exists so the business confirms, before go live, that the system meets its requirements. It should be run by users, against their requirements, and signed by the business owner. A vendor sign off dated after go live is neither acceptance nor readiness.`},
        {t:`Rerun a sample of the test cases himself on today's system.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`He'd need access he shouldn't have to a system that's changed every year since. Whatever he found would say nothing about whether it was ready in September 2019.`,
          why:`The auditor evaluates whether acceptance happened. Running the auditee's tests on today's system isn't the auditor's job, and it can't show the readiness at go live.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s11`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3B1`, title:`A copy of everyone`, q:`The test plan says where Longitude's test data came from. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`At 5:12 his phone lit up.`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="5:12 PM">home. mum asked what we had for lunch and i said tea and she said AT THE DINER? and did a face</prop>`,`<prop type="text" from="Maisie" time="5:13 PM">a good face</prop>`,`He wrote to Laura before he could think better of it. <b>Saturday, the diner, nine. I'll fetch her at eight and have her back by noon.</b> The answer came in under a minute. <b>Okay.</b> Then: <b>Thank you for telling me.</b>`,`He sat with that for a minute. Then he opened the test plan, because the eleven cases nobody ran weren't the only thing wrong with the testing, and he wanted to know what the other two hundred had been run on.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, UAT environment (extract)">Environment: hosted by Pellridge.<br>Test data: full copy of production merchant file, March 2019 (merchant names, bank account and routing numbers). Masking: none.<br>Disposal of test data after go live: not recorded.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Treat the production copy as sound practice for realistic testing.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`It is realistic. It also put every merchant's bank details into an environment run by the vendor, under none of production's protections, and nobody wrote down whether they were ever deleted.`,
          why:`Production data in testing is realistic and risky. It needs masking or protection equal to production, and a record of disposal afterward.`},
        {t:`Note it as a privacy point and leave the testing questions aside.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:1},
          after:`It is a privacy point, the same kind he'd been looking at on Tuesday. But it's also a testing control, and the copy may still exist.`,
          why:`Unmasked test data is a privacy issue and also an implementation control weakness. Record it as both, and follow the data to its disposal.`},
        {t:`Follow the unmasked test copy through to whether it was deleted.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:3,conf:0,health:5},
          after:`He writes the condition: every merchant's bank details, copied unmasked into a vendor's test environment in 2019, and no record that the copy was ever destroyed. Then he puts it beside the archive line from Pellridge's invoices, and asks counsel to ask Meridian whether the test copy still exists.`,
          why:`Test data drawn from production should be masked or protected to production's standard, and disposed of when testing ends. Without a disposal record, the organization can't show the copy is gone.`},
        {t:`Ask Pellridge for the 2019 test copy so he can rerun the tests.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`Counsel refuses on two grounds before he's finished the sentence: it's other people's bank data, held by a vendor, and he'd be making his own copy of a problem.`,
          why:`Collect only what your objective needs, through the agreed channel. Asking a vendor for a full copy of personal financial data repeats the risk you've just found.`}
      ]},
    { id:`f5s12`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`, task:`3B1`, title:`Sunday night`, q:`Longitude went live on a CFO email with acceptance pending and no rollback plan. What should Henry do?`,
      text:(s,all,G)=>[`The cutover papers were the last folder in the file. He opened them at twenty to eight, with the bicycle shop shut downstairs and the radiator knocking.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Project Longitude, go live readiness (extract)">Readiness criteria: UAT accepted by the business (status: pending). Parallel run: not planned. Rollback plan: not required.<br>Changeover: direct, over the weekend of September 14 and 15, 2019. Legacy settlement system retired Sunday night.<br>Go live approval: email, C. Wynn, Friday, September 13, 2019, 6:10 PM. "Approved. Let's go."</prop>`,`At ten past nine counsel sent the last answer of the day: the 2019 control assessment that risk register row R-07 had rested on ever since, which he'd asked for with the project file.`,`<prop type="doc" title="Control assessment, settlement dual authorization (2019)">Fieldwork: April 8 to April 19, 2019.<br>System tested: legacy settlement system.<br>Conclusion: effective.</prop>`],
      opts:[
        {t:`Leave the cutover aside as history after seven years of running.`, s:0, d:{trust:-2,conf:0,health:-4},
          after:`He writes nothing about it. The board's comfort about the capstone has rested since 2019 on a test of a system that was switched off on a Sunday night, and his file doesn't say so.`,
          why:`How a system went live bears on whether its controls were ever shown to work. And a control assessment of a replaced system says nothing about the new one.`},
        {t:`Note that it went live before the business had accepted it.`, s:1, d:{trust:0,conf:0,health:2},
          after:`It's true, and it's in the file now. But the second document tells him something bigger, and he's let it sit under the first.`,
          why:`Going live with readiness criteria unmet is a finding. The larger one is that the only control test on record predates the system that's been running since.`},
        {t:`Check which system the R-07 control test was actually run on.`, s:3, best:true, d:{trust:4,conf:0,health:6},
          after:`He puts both pages side by side. Readiness: business acceptance pending, no parallel run, no way back, approved by email on a Friday evening. Control test: April 2019, on the system that was switched off that Sunday night. Nothing in the file shows the capstone was ever tested on the system that replaced it, before go live or ever since.`,
          why:`A go live decision should be made against readiness criteria, with business acceptance and a fallback. And when a system is replaced, its key controls need testing again. Assurance about the old system doesn't carry over to the new one.`},
        {t:`Ask the CFO to re-rate R-07 tonight, before tomorrow's board.`, s:0, d:{trust:-3,conf:0,health:-3},
          after:`It would tell Meridian's CFO what he's found, the night before the board meets, and ask for a change to a record that's evidence.`,
          why:`Rating risk is management's job, and timing a request like this tells the auditee what you've found before it's reported. Record the finding and report it through your own channel.`}
      ]}
  ],
  drills:[
    {after:`f5s3`, title:`What Longitude cost, and what it promised`,
     setup:[`Longitude's business case put the one off cost at $5,000,000 and forecast savings of $2,000,000 a year. Pellridge's status report of August 30, 2019 showed planned value $4,000,000, earned value $3,200,000, and actual cost $5,600,000, under a green status. The project's final cost was $7,400,000.`],
     fields:[{label:`Payback in the business case, in years (one decimal)`,answer:2.5,tol:0.05},{label:`Cost performance index on August 30 (two decimals)`,answer:0.57,tol:0.01},{label:`Schedule performance index on August 30 (two decimals)`,answer:0.8,tol:0.01},{label:`Payback at the final cost, same savings, in years (one decimal)`,answer:3.7,tol:0.05}],
     solution:[`Payback = cost ÷ annual savings = $5,000,000 ÷ $2,000,000 = 2.5 years.`,`CPI = EV ÷ AC = $3,200,000 ÷ $5,600,000 = 0.571, or 0.57. For every dollar spent, Meridian got 57 cents of planned work. If that held, the whole project would cost $5,000,000 ÷ 0.571 = $8,750,000.`,`SPI = EV ÷ PV = $3,200,000 ÷ $4,000,000 = 0.80. Only 80 percent of the work due by the end of August was done. Both numbers were in the report. The circle at the top was green anyway.`,`Payback at the final cost = $7,400,000 ÷ $2,000,000 = 3.7 years, and the cost overran by ($7,400,000 minus $5,000,000) ÷ $5,000,000 = 48 percent. The project finished well under the $8,750,000 the trend pointed to, and the deferred list shows how: work was moved to a release 2 that never came. And the $2,000,000 a year was never measured, so even 3.7 years is a forecast.`]}
  ],
  exercise: { after:`f5s10`, title:`Which test?`, intro:`Dot's notebook had a page headed "Who tested what" with four columns. Put each of these on the right one.`,
    items:[
      {prompt:`A developer checks the new fee calculation routine on its own, with good and bad inputs.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:0, why:`One piece of code, tested alone, by the person who built it. Unit testing.`},
      {prompt:`Testers confirm that files from the settlement application arrive complete at the release gateway.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:1, why:`Data passing between two components. Integration testing.`},
      {prompt:`Settlement staff run a week of their own scenarios against the requirements, and their manager signs the results.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:2, why:`The business, testing against its own requirements, signing before go live. User acceptance testing.`},
      {prompt:`After a fix to the approval screen, the team reruns last month's tests to make sure nothing else broke.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:3, why:`Old tests rerun after a change. Regression testing.`},
      {prompt:`The check digit routine for bank account numbers is tested by itself before it joins the rest of the system.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:0, why:`A single routine, tested in isolation. Unit testing.`},
      {prompt:`The head of settlement operations confirms the system does what the business asked for, before it goes live.`, choices:[`Unit`,`Integration`,`User acceptance`,`Regression`], answer:2, why:`The business owner accepting the system against business requirements. User acceptance, and the sign off Longitude never had.`}
    ]},
  quiz: [
    {q:`Which group should decide whether a troubled system development project continues, is changed, or stops?`, opts:[`The vendor's project manager`,`The development team leads`,`The steering committee`,`The internal audit function`], a:2, why:`The steering committee, with the sponsor, makes the major project decisions. The project manager runs the work and reports to it. The auditor evaluates and advises.`, task:`3A1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`In a project schedule, what is TRUE of tasks on the critical path?`, opts:[`They carry the most cost`,`They have no slack`,`They need the most staff`,`They come first in the plan`], a:1, why:`The critical path is the longest chain of dependent tasks, and its tasks have zero slack. A delay on any of them delays the project.`, task:`3A1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A project has earned value of $300,000 and actual cost of $400,000. What is its cost performance index?`, opts:[`1.33`,`0.75`,`0.25`,`1.00`], a:1, why:`CPI = EV ÷ AC = $300,000 ÷ $400,000 = 0.75. Below 1 means the project is getting less work than it's paying for. 1.33 divides the wrong way round.`, task:`3A1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A project costs $900,000 and is expected to save $300,000 a year. What is its payback period?`, opts:[`2 years`,`4 years`,`6 years`,`3 years`], a:3, why:`Payback = cost ÷ annual savings = $900,000 ÷ $300,000 = 3 years.`, task:`3A2`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`After a new system goes live, who should be responsible for measuring whether its forecast benefits were achieved?`, opts:[`The vendor that built it`,`The IS auditor`,`The IT operations team`,`The business sponsor`], a:3, why:`The sponsor owns the business case and the benefits it promised, so the business measures them against the forecast. The auditor can check that this happened.`, task:`3A2`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`In a scrum project, who represents the business and decides the order of the product backlog?`, opts:[`The product owner`,`The scrum master`,`The lead developer`,`The project sponsor`], a:0, why:`The product owner speaks for the business and stakeholders and prioritizes the backlog. The scrum master supports the process, and developers decide how to build.`, task:`3A3`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`For which kind of project is a traditional waterfall approach BEST suited?`, opts:[`One where users will only know what they need once they see it`,`One with stable, well understood requirements`,`One that must release changes several times a day`,`One where the scope is expected to change often`], a:1, why:`Waterfall finishes each phase before the next, so it works best when requirements are stable and understood up front. Changing or unclear requirements suit iterative methods.`, task:`3A3`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A vendor develops a critical application for a company and keeps ownership of the source code. Which control BEST protects the company if the vendor fails?`, opts:[`A source code escrow agreement`,`A longer software license term`,`Annual vendor satisfaction surveys`,`A clause fixing the license price`], a:0, why:`Escrow places the code with a third party, to be released to the customer if the vendor fails or stops supporting it. The other terms don't give the company the code.`, task:`3A3`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A key system control was dropped from a release, approved by the vendor's project manager, with a manual procedure named as the compensating control. What is the auditor's MAIN concern?`, opts:[`The release notes didn't mention the change`,`The vendor's fee should have been lowered`,`The procedure is not written in enough detail`,`No business owner with authority accepted the risk`], a:3, why:`Deferring a key control is a risk acceptance, which belongs to the business owner who carries the risk. A vendor's project manager can't accept it for the business.`, task:`3A4`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A payment system's approval threshold is stored as a configuration setting. Which control is MOST important over that setting?`, opts:[`Showing the threshold on the approval screen`,`Setting it to a round number`,`Authorizing and logging every change to it`,`Reviewing its value once a year`], a:2, why:`A control that depends on a setting is only as strong as the protection over it. Changes should be authorized and logged where the person making them can't alter the log.`, task:`3A4`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`What is the PRIMARY purpose of user acceptance testing?`, opts:[`To confirm that each code module works alone`,`To confirm the system meets business requirements`,`To measure performance under peak load`,`To check that interfaces pass data correctly`], a:1, why:`UAT is the business confirming, before go live, that the system meets its requirements. Module tests are unit testing, peak load is performance or stress testing, and interfaces are integration testing.`, task:`3B1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`Which changeover approach puts a new system into use at one location first, before rolling it out everywhere?`, opts:[`Parallel`,`Phased`,`Pilot`,`Direct`], a:2, why:`A pilot runs the new system in one place first. Parallel runs old and new side by side, phased moves one piece at a time, and direct switches over all at once.`, task:`3B1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`Which type of testing confirms that separately built components exchange data correctly?`, opts:[`Integration testing`,`Unit testing`,`Regression testing`,`Acceptance testing`], a:0, why:`Integration testing checks the interfaces between components. Unit testing checks pieces alone, regression rechecks after changes, and acceptance testing checks business requirements.`, task:`3B1`, domain:`Acquisition and Implementation`},
    {q:`A risk register rates a risk low because of a control test done before the system was replaced. What should the IS auditor conclude?`, opts:[`The rating has no current support`,`The rating holds until the next review`,`The risk should be rated high now`,`The new system must be riskier`], a:0, why:`A residual rating depends on controls working, and a test of the old system says nothing about the new one. The rating has no current support. Re-rating it is management's job. Review of chapter 3.`, task:`2A5`, domain:`Governance and Management of IT`},
    {q:`The auditee won't release records the IS auditor needs for a key test, and the report is due. What should the auditor do?`, opts:[`Leave that test out of the final report`,`Rely on the auditee's own summary of them`,`Hold the report back until they arrive`,`Report the limitation and its effect`], a:3, why:`When access is restricted, the report states the limitation, what couldn't be tested, and how it affects the conclusions. Leaving it out overstates the work. Review of chapter 3.`, task:`1B5`, domain:`Auditing Process`}
  ],
  closing:(s,all,G)=>{const P=s.picks||{};
    return [
    ...(P[7]===2
      ?[`At 8:15 an email had come in from Leonard, copied to nobody. <b>Sent Carla the plan with a note saying IA shouldn't own the design or sign it off. She's given it to the CIO. Don't know if that's better. It's more correct. L.</b> He filed it with the others, with the time.`]
      :P[7]===1
      ?[`At seven Leonard's draft had arrived, eleven pages, with a covering line thanking him for agreeing to look. Henry hadn't opened it. He forwarded it to counsel unread, with a note saying so, which was the best he could do with it now.`]
      :P[7]===0
      ?[`Somewhere in Meridian's building, Leonard was typing up a design for tomorrow's board that Henry had dictated most of across a diner table. In a month he'd have to write about whether it worked.`]
      :[]),
    (P[11]===2
      ?`R-07 was already in the file in one sentence, with both dates and the source of each. He read it once and didn't change a word.`
      :`It was nearly ten before he took R-07 out of Friday's file and laid it next to the assessment, and saw what the two dates said together. The test had been done on the old system. The old system had been switched off five months later, and nobody had tested the control on the new one. Ever since, the board had been told the risk was low on the strength of a test of a system that no longer existed. He wrote it in, late, with both dates.`),
    `On Friday he'd left himself a question beside that row. The platform had been built in 2019. Before, or after? He found the page and wrote the answer under it. Before.`,
    `At nine tomorrow the directors would open the appendix nobody had numbered and find LOW, where it had been since 2019.`
  ]},
  episode:{src:`audio/full-ch05.mp3`, len:`about 45 minutes`},
  next:`Change Window`
});
